  "query_preview": "term, but this doesn't become a significant issue for our\nlocal forecast until the later part of next week. All the global\nmodels vary slightly in timing and location of a mid-upper level\ntrough and associated surface low swinging across the CONUS,\nbringing the attendant surface front towards Florida mid to late\nnext week. Prior to that, Florida remains under the influence of\npersistent troughing over the eastern seaboard and surface high\npressure in the western Atlantic. There is some disagreement in\nthe exact location of the surface ridge axis, but fairly confident\nlow level flow will remain onshore, just a question of whether it\nwill be more easterly or southerly. There is less certainty how\nmuch moisture will recover, with a fair spread between PWATs\nticking up to near to below normal or surging back to above\nnormal, decreasing confidence in forecast rain chances and heat\nimpacts. As a result, official forecast calls for broadly\nincreasing rain chances from near/below normal Tuesday, becoming\nnear/above normal Wednesday, with the return of lightning storm\nchances. Temperatures tick up a few degrees becoming seasonably\nwarm again in the M-U80s, but the additional moisture causing heat\nindices to jump back into the 90s.",
